{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["After he sent her away — after Moshe returned her and sent her away from Egypt, for we have not seen until now that he sent her back to his father-in-law's house. And some interpret: and afterwards he sent her dowry to her, as it is written: שילוחים לבת פרעה. And the plain sense appears to be that we find no verses on this matter, but rather that before Yitro sent her and her children to him in the wilderness, he should have written it above, after the pericope of the blood, the circumcision; and afterwards Moshe again sent her to her father — her and her children — in the manner of וחם הוא אבי כנען." "After he sent her away — like: ואחר תטהר. And as I explained: אחר נאחז בסבך. קדמו שרים אחר נוגנים."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "Rashbam addresses the chronological problem: Scripture mentions Moshe having sent Tziporrah away, but the narrative of that sending has not appeared. His solution involves the anticipation principle (the text refers back to an event that should have been narrated earlier) and the comparison to וחם הוא אבי כנען (Genesis 9:18), a parenthetical remark placed mid-narrative.", 1 "Rashbam cites three instances of אחר followed by a construct-state form to show the grammatical pattern; the second and third are cross-references to his own comments elsewhere, the third apparently from Judges 5:12."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:41:58.576274841Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}